    dot pt_peak at.data [24,4.82] cites ["bls_eggs"] hidden true
    label lab_peak "$4.82" right attach_to pt_peak hidden true
    scratch card work ["Eggs:  P ↑   Q ↓","Roses:  P ↑   Q ↑"] x 850 y 530 font_size 30 hidden true
    text two_ways "Move along, or shift?" #2f6b8f x 850 y 400 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Eggs and roses"

What happens to a market when buyers suddenly want more, or sellers suddenly can offer less? Start with a price everyone noticed: American eggs.
    draw axes

This is the average price of a dozen large eggs in US cities, month by month.
    draw line_1

Through 2021 it stayed under a dollar eighty.
    draw pt_low lab_low

Then, in February 2022 (@draw flu_pt flu_tag@), bird flu reached American farms. By the end of that year, more than forty-three million laying hens had died or been culled.
    animate stage 1 duration 4

The price climbed, and in January 2023 it hit about four dollars eighty: almost three times the old level.
    draw pt_peak lab_peak

    pause 0.4

B: And people got fewer eggs for it, I assume?
    point at.data [2022.9,4.6] gesture circle

Yes: prod (@draw card_box card_line_1@)uction at the end of 2022 was about six percent below a year earlier. As the flocks w (@animate stage 2 duration 3@)ere rebuilt, the price fell back to about two dollars by the summer of 2023.

Price up, quantity down. That fits the rule everyone knows: dearer things sell less.
    highlight card_line_1

B: Then explain roses. The week before Valentine's Day they cost more than in any ordinary week, and florists sell far more of them.
    draw card_line_2

B: Price up, quantity up. Did the rule just break?
    highlight card_line_1 card_line_2

No. The two price rises have different causes. To see them, we need to tell apart two things that sound alike: a move along (@draw two_ways@) a curve, and a shift of the whole curve.
    erase flu_pt flu_tag

## Shift or slide
vars: {"qa":50}
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Quantity of eggs (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand: {}
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    supply_shift:
      amount: 0
      label: S′
    change_arrows: true

    card title "Shift or slide"

    hide supply_shift_arrow

B: Two price rises with opposite quantities. Let's draw the egg market and find out why.
    draw axes

B: The demand curve, D, shows how many eggs buyers want at each price (@draw supply_curve label_S@). The supply curve, S, how many farms offer.
    draw demand_curve label_D

B: Where they cross sits the market price, P star, and the quantity sold, Q star.
    draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar

So if the price of eggs goes up, the demand curve moves?
    highlight demand_curve

B: No, and that's the trap. When the egg's own  (@animate qa 32 duration 2.5@)price changes, buyers move along their curve. The curve stays put.
    dot slide #b5482e at.on demand_curve at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"qa"}

B: Anything else that changes what people want, or what f (@highlight supply_curve@)arms can offer at every price, shifts the whole curve.
    erase slide

B: So: bird flu kills millions of hens. Which curve moves, and which way?
    highlight supply_curve demand_curve

    ask "Where does supply go? Move it, then press Done." on supply_curve predict true store t voice b
        right: Yes. {t.why}
        wrong: Not quite. {t.why}

B: Supply  (@animate supply_shift.amount -25 duration 3@)shifts left: at every price, farms can now offer fewer eggs.
    draw supply_shift_curve label_S_shift

    fade supply_curve label_S to 0.35

B: At the old price, buyers still want as many eggs as before, but there are far fewer to sell, so the price is bid up.
    point at.data [40,50] gesture circle

B: As it rises, buyers slide u (@highlight demand_curve@)p along their unchanged demand curve, and some buy fewer eggs.
    draw shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift

So the new crossing has a higher price and a smaller quantity: suppl (@draw change_arrows@)y shifting pushes them in opposite directions.
    draw shift_guide_lines label_P_shift label_Q_shift

B: And the buyers' demand curve n (@highlight demand_curve label_D@)ever moved. Only where they stood on it did.
    erase change_arrows

## When both move
vars: {"sh":0}
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Quantity (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand: {}
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    demand_shift:
      amount: 0
      label: D′
sources:
    - id: working1927
      title: What Do Statistical "Demand Curves" Show?
      authors: E. J. Working
      year: 1927
      finding: 'Observed price–quantity points come from shifts of both supply and demand, so a line fitted through them need be neither a demand nor a supply curve: the identification problem. (Quarterly Journal of Economics 41(2): 212–235.)'

    path s2 #1f4e6b points [[2,8],[96,92]] data true bind {"points.0.0":"max(2+sh,1)","points.0.1":"8+84*(max(2+sh,1)-2-sh)/94","points.1.0":"min(96+sh,99)","points.1.1":"8+84*(min(96+sh,99)-2-sh)/94"} style.stroke_width 4.5 hidden true
    label label_s2 "S′" right #1f4e6b attach_to s2 hidden true
    dot cross #3d3833 at.data [61.5,61.17] bind {"at.data.0":"(123+sh)/2","at.data.1":"8+84*((123+sh)/2-2-sh)/94"} hidden true
    label label_cross "E′" above attach_to cross hidden true
    scratch rule top-right work ["D shifts: P, Q together","S shifts: P, Q apart"] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot obs_1 #8a5fa8 at.data [38,47] hidden true
    dot obs_2 #8a5fa8 at.data [46,58] hidden true
    dot obs_3 #8a5fa8 at.data [57,55] hidden true
    dot obs_4 #8a5fa8 at.data [64,66] hidden true
    path obs_line dashed #8a5fa8 points [[30,44],[72,68]] data true hidden true
    text obs_tag "Data: points, not curves" #8a5fa8 cites ["working1927"] x 740 y 615 font_size 30 hidden true
    text sure "Logic: consensus" #2f6b8f x 740 y 615 font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch math top-right work [{"tex":"Q_d = a - bP \\quad Q_s = c + dP"},{"tex":"P^* = \\frac{a-c}{b+d} \\qquad Q^* = \\frac{ad+bc}{b+d}"},"a ↑ :  P* ↑  Q* ↑","c ↓ :  P* ↑  Q* ↓"] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "When both move"

    hide shift_equilibrium_point shift_guide_lines label_E_shift label_P_shift label_Q_shift demand_shift_arrow

A supply shift pushes price and quantity apart. The roses need the other curve.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar

In Valentine's week, deman (@animate demand_shift.amount 25 duration 3@)d for roses shifts right: at every price, people want more.
    draw demand_shift_curve label_D_shift

The new crossing is higher and further right. A demand (@fade demand_curve label_D to 0.35@) shift moves price and quantity in the same direction.
    draw cross label_cross

B: Try it yourself: drag the demand curve right or left, and watch price and quantity move together.
    explore

So the direction is a clue: moving together points to d (@draw rule_line_2@)emand, moving apart points to supply.
    draw rule_box rule_line_1

B: But real markets rarely move one curve at a time. What if both shift at once?
    erase rule

Say demand for roses rises while  (@animate sh -20 duration 3@)a cold spell on the flower farms also cuts supply.
    draw s2 label_s2

    fade supply_curve label_S to 0.35

The price surely rises. The quantity could go either way (@highlight cross label_Qstar@): it depends on which shift is bigger.
    animate sh -38 duration 3

B: And in the data, nobody ever sees a curve. On (@draw obs_1 obs_2 obs_3 obs_4@)ly the crossing points, one per week or year.
    fade demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S demand_shift_curve label_D_shift s2 label_s2 cross label_cross guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar equilibrium_point label_E to 0.12

B: It is an old trap: in 1927 the economist Elmer Working showed that a line through such points can be neither a dem (@draw obs_tag@)and curve nor a supply curve. Economists call it the identification problem.
    draw obs_line

How sure are we? About the lo (@draw sure@)gic of shifts, there is consensus. Measuring which curve moved, and by how much, is harder: it needs an outside event, like the weather, that moves only one of them.
    erase obs_tag obs_line

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test

    ask "Want the algebra behind the rule?" on mathq stage own

@math

    erase obs_1 obs_2 obs_3 obs_4 sure mathq

Write demand and supply as straight lines: quantity demanded falls with the price, quantity supplied rises with it.
    draw math_box math_line_1

Set them equal and solve, and you get the price and the quantity where they cross.
    draw math_line_2

B: Raise a, more demand at every price, and both go up.
    draw math_line_3

Cut c, less supply at every price, and the price rises while the quantity falls.
    draw math_line_4

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq obs_1 obs_2 obs_3 obs_4 sure

    fade demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S demand_shift_curve label_D_shift s2 label_s2 cross label_cross guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar equilibrium_point label_E to 1

    quiz "A storm keeps Harbourtown's fishing boats in port for a week. What happens to the price of fish and the amount sold?"
        right: Price up, amount down: the storm cuts the supply of fish, so they move apart.
        wrong: Did the storm change what buyers want, or what sellers can offer?
        + Price up, amount down
        * Price up, amount up
        * Price down, amount down

    quiz "A summer festival fills Harbourtown with mainland visitors. What happens at Mia's bakery?" voice b
        right: Price up, more bread sold: the visitors shift demand right, so both rise together.
        wrong: The visitors change how much bread is wanted at every price.
        + Price up, more bread sold
        * Price up, less bread sold
        * Price down, more bread sold

    quiz "Bread gets dearer, and Harbourtown families buy less bread. What is that, for the families?"
        right: A move along their demand curve: only bread's own price changed.
        wrong: Did anything change for the families except bread's own price?
        * Their demand curve shifts left
        + A move along their demand curve
        * Their supply curve shifts right

    quiz "A cheaper oven cuts Mia's costs just as a sourdough craze sweeps the town. What can we say for sure?" voice b
        right: The amount sold rises: both changes push it up, while the price is pulled both ways.
        wrong: Both changes push the quantity the same way. Do they push the price the same way?
        * The price rises
        + The amount sold rises
        * Both rise
        * Neither changes

So when a price jumps, ask first what moved: what buyers want, or what sellers can offer. If one curve moved, that tells you which way the quantity goes; if both did, it depends on which push was bigger.
    highlight demand_shift_curve s2

